      "own": "0dfe923a99c9aa54"
    },
    "notes": [
      {
        "by": "Ada",
        "date": "2026-11-02",
        "text": "Why define this as ∃ k, n = d * k rather than n % d = 0?"
      },
      {
        "by": "assistant",
        "date": "2026-11-02",
        "text": "They agree for every d and n, but the existential form is what proofs need: to show d divides n you give the k, and from a proof you get the k back. It is also how Mathlib defines divisibility."
      },
      {
        "by": "Ada",
        "date": "2026-11-02",
        "text": "So 0 divides only 0, and every number divides 0?"
      },
      {
        "by": "assistant",
        "date": "2026-11-02",
        "text": "Yes. Both are now theorems: Divides.zero (every d divides 0) in this module, and Divides.zero_left (0 divides n exactly when n = 0) in ReviewExample.Zero."
      }
    ],
    "status": "agree"
  },
